00:42:22,311 --> 00:42:24,492
[SPEAKER_02]: I have something here about cigars and the bread box.

632
00:42:25,513 --> 00:42:27,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes.

633
00:42:27,335 --> 00:42:34,200
[SPEAKER_00]: We were sitting him there coughing, and she started going into the bread box, and she said,

634
00:42:35,637 --> 00:42:37,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, she said, I'm not here for something new.

635
00:42:37,578 --> 00:42:39,798
[SPEAKER_00]: She said, this is where I hide my cigarettes.

636
00:42:40,958 --> 00:42:44,159
[SPEAKER_00]: She kept them in her front box or cartons of cigarettes.

637
00:42:46,380 --> 00:42:47,820
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that was hilarious.

638
00:42:49,401 --> 00:42:50,401
[SPEAKER_02]: What did you think of the house?

639
00:42:51,741 --> 00:42:52,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it was a lovely house.

640
00:42:53,382 --> 00:42:55,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It was, it was money.

641
00:42:55,322 --> 00:42:56,063
[SPEAKER_00]: How old were you then?

642
00:42:58,063 --> 00:42:59,083
[SPEAKER_00]: The house itself?

643
00:43:00,104 --> 00:43:03,384
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd say it was probably 20, 25, 30 years old.

644
00:43:03,404 --> 00:43:05,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe it here for your benefit.

645
00:43:05,703 --> 00:43:10,085
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always a one-floor plan, like all these things are.

646
00:43:10,105 --> 00:43:32,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You go into a little foyer, and off to the right is the living room, and off to the left is the dining room, and then behind the living room is this floor to room, and the kitchen is off that, to the left, and then the bedrooms are their bedrooms at the back, and then they have two other bedrooms in that house.

647
00:43:35,064 --> 00:43:46,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Then off to the right of the Florida room is this room where Al has all his pictures on, Tony has all his pictures on, and then a garage in front of that.

648
00:43:46,293 --> 00:43:48,395
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the addition that he had built on.

649
00:43:49,035 --> 00:43:51,978
[SPEAKER_00]: That would normally have gone out in the garage that door.

650
00:43:55,601 --> 00:43:57,662
[SPEAKER_00]: But the whole thing reeked of money.

651
00:43:59,043 --> 00:43:59,804
[SPEAKER_00]: You can tell.

652
00:44:03,569 --> 00:44:10,330
[SPEAKER_00]: beautiful fabric covering all the chairs, and the plush carpet, and everything was just so in that house.

653
00:44:10,350 --> 00:44:14,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Were there pictures of players or drawings of players framed around the room?

654
00:44:15,591 --> 00:44:16,532
[SPEAKER_00]: No, some of him.

655
00:44:16,552 --> 00:44:17,672
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what it was.

656
00:44:18,012 --> 00:44:18,832
[SPEAKER_00]: There weren't pictures of him.

657
00:44:18,852 --> 00:44:21,293
[SPEAKER_02]: There weren't pictures always, there were drawings of him.

658
00:44:21,333 --> 00:44:22,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but they were of him.

659
00:44:24,253 --> 00:44:26,214
[SPEAKER_00]: They were of him, that one oil portrait.

660
00:44:26,874 --> 00:44:32,315
[SPEAKER_00]: They had a Mother of the Year award and a Mother-Father of the Year award up there, and then they had

661
00:44:33,408 --> 00:44:44,597
[SPEAKER_00]: pictures of their children, a lot of pictures of their children out there, and some baseball pro people in that Florida room.

662
00:44:44,737 --> 00:44:49,361
[SPEAKER_02]: This Tony Cuccinella struck me as a very nervous person.

663
00:44:50,062 --> 00:45:01,091
[SPEAKER_02]: As you may have gathered in the interview, he started talking at about 60 miles an hour almost, and I wondered just where he would get through his career before he even had the tape recorder on.

664
00:45:02,672 --> 00:45:14,702
[SPEAKER_02]: He was very nervous, and as Rita said, he seemed to have a prepared speech there, something like Tony Pyatt did back in, well, he read his back in Chicago.

665
00:45:15,762 --> 00:45:31,615
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think I slowed him down a little bit, but he still was quite quick and kind of impulsive in talking, and he said what he had to say very quickly,

666
00:45:32,828 --> 00:45:38,331
[SPEAKER_02]: and earnestly, and I didn't sense much of a sense of humor in what he had to say.

667
00:45:38,391 --> 00:45:48,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Occasionally he'd start chuckling about something that obviously he needed chuckling about, but he's very serious, and this is all business.

668
00:45:49,177 --> 00:45:52,659
[SPEAKER_02]: And there goes an airplane overhead.

669
00:45:53,599 --> 00:45:57,581
[SPEAKER_00]: He can do more traffic than a Washington airplane.

670
00:45:57,742 --> 00:45:59,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you sense that a little bit?

671
00:45:59,362 --> 00:46:00,383
[SPEAKER_00]: They're both like that.

672
00:46:01,075 --> 00:46:11,358
[SPEAKER_00]: I think they're both very hypertension and I think they're both very nervous and they're just very quick emotional giants.

673
00:46:11,998 --> 00:46:16,879
[SPEAKER_00]: I imagine that when they really get going you're probably a virile laugh.

674
00:46:17,699 --> 00:46:25,881
[SPEAKER_02]: Every question I ask he came right back real quickly with a very full detail but a staccato almost like response.

675
00:46:26,541 --> 00:46:28,342
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think it's just their nature.

676
00:46:29,110 --> 00:46:31,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Because she talked up the storm.

677
00:46:32,832 --> 00:46:33,732
[SPEAKER_00]: The telephone rang?

678
00:46:34,052 --> 00:46:35,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the telephone rang.

679
00:46:35,753 --> 00:46:47,918
[SPEAKER_00]: And she was carrying on this conversation with this Doris, who was talking about her roles and her Cadillac and everything, and where they were going to take it to get it fixed.

680
00:46:48,218 --> 00:46:51,860
[SPEAKER_00]: And Doris wanted to go out, and then she started asking me questions.

681
00:46:51,880 --> 00:46:53,821
[SPEAKER_00]: What were some of those?

682
00:46:53,841 --> 00:46:57,282
[SPEAKER_02]: She wanted to know... What did Doris want to know?

683
00:46:57,422 --> 00:46:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Doris wanted to know...

684
00:47:00,746 --> 00:47:14,236
[SPEAKER_00]: what Gene did, where he taught, what he taught, and all this is being relayed on the phone from me to Mrs. Doris.

685
00:47:15,637 --> 00:47:21,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And Doris says, oh, she said, yes, his wife is in here with me, and I just feel like I've known her for years.

686
00:47:24,624 --> 00:47:24,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

687
00:47:26,563 --> 00:47:32,168
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the window man came, which kind of ended our conversation, because she was trotting around there.

688
00:47:32,548 --> 00:47:34,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But every year they've lived down there.

689
00:47:34,630 --> 00:47:41,875
[SPEAKER_00]: She goes grocery shopping on Thursday and gets her hair done on Friday, and the only time she ever canceled it was when Kennedy was assassinated.

690
00:47:41,915 --> 00:47:42,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Why was that?

691
00:47:42,656 --> 00:47:43,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Did she know something?

692
00:47:43,637 --> 00:47:46,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Because she had gotten this letter from her daughter.

693
00:47:46,559 --> 00:47:48,120
[SPEAKER_00]: It had some stupendous news in it.

694
00:47:48,160 --> 00:47:50,722
[SPEAKER_00]: I wonder if she was coming home or something like that.

695
00:47:50,742 --> 00:47:51,583
[SPEAKER_02]: She was a nun by then.

696
00:47:51,723 --> 00:47:52,343
[SPEAKER_02]: A nun, yeah.

697
00:47:52,384 --> 00:47:53,825
[SPEAKER_02]: She'd been a nun for many years.

698
00:47:53,845 --> 00:47:54,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

699
00:47:56,803 --> 00:47:58,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, does that wrap up Tony Cooch?

700
00:47:58,524 --> 00:47:59,685
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that wraps up Tony Cooch.

