769
00:53:08,016 --> 00:53:09,898
[SPEAKER_00]: My stocking cap.

770
00:53:10,358 --> 00:53:11,539
[SPEAKER_00]: I had changed in the walk-off.

771
00:53:11,579 --> 00:53:13,180
[SPEAKER_02]: It was cold last night.

772
00:53:19,563 --> 00:53:21,565
[SPEAKER_02]: I was down about my first sweater by this time.

773
00:53:23,106 --> 00:53:28,030
[SPEAKER_02]: And... You know, I haven't been out of these clothes for two days.

774
00:53:28,791 --> 00:53:29,651
[SPEAKER_02]: You didn't have to.

775
00:53:29,852 --> 00:53:30,352
[SPEAKER_02]: You changed.

776
00:53:30,412 --> 00:53:31,293
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I didn't change.

777
00:53:31,573 --> 00:53:32,694
[SPEAKER_02]: I just changed my pants.

778
00:53:32,774 --> 00:53:33,995
[SPEAKER_02]: I got my underwear still on.

779
00:53:34,015 --> 00:53:35,536
[SPEAKER_02]: I got to take a shower and I'm reeking.

780
00:53:37,118 --> 00:53:39,640
[SPEAKER_02]: We both reek, though, so we don't notice it.

781
00:53:41,321 --> 00:53:47,166
[SPEAKER_02]: And so we got on the road after our egg foo young down Route 41 to the fifth stoplight.

782
00:53:47,576 --> 00:53:56,058
[SPEAKER_02]: And of course I turned off at the fourth stoplight because that said follow route 41 and 41 went left at the fourth stoplight.

783
00:53:56,078 --> 00:53:58,158
[SPEAKER_02]: It took a half hour to get back on the right road.

784
00:53:58,678 --> 00:54:03,379
[SPEAKER_02]: The traffic was bumper to bumper for almost a couple hundred yards.

785
00:54:03,399 --> 00:54:07,460
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd have to wait two or three changes in the light.

786
00:54:08,080 --> 00:54:09,000
[SPEAKER_02]: I had to make the turn.

787
00:54:09,140 --> 00:54:13,901
[SPEAKER_02]: I realized I was in the wrong lane before I ever got to the second change of light.

788
00:54:15,502 --> 00:54:16,622
[SPEAKER_02]: So I had to swing around.

789
00:54:17,520 --> 00:54:26,026
[SPEAKER_02]: Made it back through, I cheated though, I cut through a shopping center and saved about 20 seconds, I think, to get back there.

790
00:54:26,766 --> 00:54:32,170
[SPEAKER_02]: And we got back on the right road and went down to the fifth stoplight.

791
00:54:34,192 --> 00:54:35,312
[SPEAKER_02]: And I told Milt Gasper...

792
00:54:35,332 --> 00:54:37,194
[SPEAKER_00]: When are my ginger ales all gone?

793
00:54:37,414 --> 00:54:39,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, what are you drinking my ginger ale for?

794
00:54:39,355 --> 00:54:40,556
[SPEAKER_02]: You see me drinking it here?

795
00:54:40,776 --> 00:54:41,256
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

796
00:54:42,077 --> 00:54:43,298
[SPEAKER_00]: When did I drink that?

797
00:54:43,558 --> 00:54:44,299
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

798
00:54:45,439 --> 00:54:46,320
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm glad you saw me.

799
00:54:47,410 --> 00:54:53,092
[SPEAKER_02]: And I said we'd be there about three because we'd have to eat and then find a motel or a campground.

800
00:54:53,912 --> 00:54:57,433
[SPEAKER_02]: We couldn't find a motel and we ate so we went there and got there about 2.30.

801
00:54:57,933 --> 00:55:03,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Went to the fifth light and it was the Oak Villas and we couldn't find it.

802
00:55:03,835 --> 00:55:06,796
[SPEAKER_02]: We went around the villa round and round in there.

803
00:55:07,436 --> 00:55:08,936
[SPEAKER_02]: These are houses behind houses.

804
00:55:10,577 --> 00:55:12,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Finally some lady told us where it was.

805
00:55:12,977 --> 00:55:14,978
[SPEAKER_02]: And there was milk coming out tall,

806
00:55:16,762 --> 00:55:30,329
[SPEAKER_02]: slim, trim, energetic, looking a little bit like 83, maybe, but full of vigor and pleasant and outgoing and in full control of everything.

807
00:55:30,909 --> 00:55:36,972
[SPEAKER_02]: And he invited us in and we had a very delightful... Oh, it must have been about two hours there, I suppose.

808
00:55:37,052 --> 00:55:38,532
[SPEAKER_02]: I got about an hour and a half tape.

809
00:55:39,413 --> 00:55:44,175
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a little bit left on it, but I thought we'd fill in the comments on this tape now and make this a

810
00:55:44,793 --> 00:55:49,199
[SPEAKER_02]: a comment tape, since we've got a little bit of comment space left.

811
00:55:50,201 --> 00:55:50,701
[SPEAKER_02]: Comment.

812
00:55:51,422 --> 00:55:54,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't you say it louder?

813
00:55:55,508 --> 00:55:58,953
[SPEAKER_00]: She went in to watch a soap opera just after we got there.

814
00:56:02,075 --> 00:56:04,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Their house was nice, but it was much smaller.

815
00:56:04,876 --> 00:56:08,857
[SPEAKER_02]: They had the TV on in the room we were talking with the soap operas on.

816
00:56:08,897 --> 00:56:11,578
[SPEAKER_02]: He shut it off and sent her off into the other room.

817
00:56:11,598 --> 00:56:12,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, she went into the other room.

818
00:56:12,638 --> 00:56:16,799
[SPEAKER_02]: She said, let's turn this thing off.

819
00:56:16,859 --> 00:56:18,459
[SPEAKER_02]: I would have asked them to turn it off anyhow.

820
00:56:18,660 --> 00:56:23,841
[SPEAKER_00]: It was a nice house, but their furniture was a little threadbare and worn and everything.

821
00:56:24,321 --> 00:56:27,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Apparently they don't have any family.

822
00:56:28,662 --> 00:56:31,163
[SPEAKER_00]: She always went to spring training with them, except

823
00:56:32,013 --> 00:56:44,163
[SPEAKER_00]: But she didn't go on the road with him because she went between his family who lived in New Jersey and his sister in Boston.

824
00:56:45,083 --> 00:56:48,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Those were the two she visited.

825
00:56:49,147 --> 00:56:51,288
[SPEAKER_00]: She was very pleasant when she came back out.

826
00:56:51,308 --> 00:56:53,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Got us a Coke, got Gina a beer.

827
00:56:53,770 --> 00:56:55,411
[SPEAKER_02]: I left half a beer.

828
00:56:55,431 --> 00:56:57,053
[SPEAKER_02]: That was the biggest beer I ever saw.

829
00:56:57,807 --> 00:56:59,968
[SPEAKER_00]: A. Yeah, it was one of those big ones.

830
00:57:00,109 --> 00:57:02,190
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the one they split, but I had this habit.

831
00:57:02,330 --> 00:57:04,992
[SPEAKER_00]: I just took care of it.

832
00:57:05,632 --> 00:57:07,553
[SPEAKER_02]: When I went in there, he showed me this.

833
00:57:08,614 --> 00:57:10,335
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how it happened.

834
00:57:10,355 --> 00:57:12,816
[SPEAKER_00]: M. You said you were a Cleveland Indian fan.

835
00:57:13,037 --> 00:57:13,257
[SPEAKER_00]: A.

836
00:57:13,317 --> 00:57:14,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Did I tell him on the phone, I wonder?

837
00:57:14,958 --> 00:57:17,599
[SPEAKER_00]: M. No, you said that as soon as you went in.

838
00:57:17,960 --> 00:57:18,020
[SPEAKER_02]: A.

839
00:57:18,060 --> 00:57:20,541
[SPEAKER_02]: He came up awfully quickly with a Cleveland Indian scrapbook.

840
00:57:20,561 --> 00:57:21,742
[SPEAKER_00]: M. And you said something about

841
00:57:22,485 --> 00:57:26,448
[SPEAKER_00]: You were born in 21 and you'd always heard about the 1921 World Series.

842
00:57:26,488 --> 00:57:27,569
[SPEAKER_02]: I said that much later.

843
00:57:27,929 --> 00:57:30,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you said you were a Cleveland Indian.

844
00:57:30,431 --> 00:57:31,531
[SPEAKER_00]: He had showed me that.

845
00:57:31,551 --> 00:57:33,953
[SPEAKER_02]: The first thing he showed me was this little scrapbook.

846
00:57:33,973 --> 00:57:35,114
[SPEAKER_02]: I've got it sitting over here.

847
00:57:35,134 --> 00:57:38,716
[SPEAKER_02]: A lovely little scrapbook of the 1920 World Series, I'd say.

848
00:57:40,097 --> 00:57:46,602
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd say 8 by 10, bound in hard...bound in leather.

849
00:57:47,279 --> 00:57:59,083
[SPEAKER_02]: And inside it says, this sports scrapbook compiled and issued May 1st, 1953 to commemorate the 25th birthday of True Fit Screw Products Corporation.

850
00:58:01,203 --> 00:58:04,684
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's presented to Milt Gaston, compliments of an admirer.

851
00:58:05,885 --> 00:58:08,726
[SPEAKER_02]: And I just leaf through, this is about 50 pages.

852
00:58:08,806 --> 00:58:12,547
[SPEAKER_02]: It has the story, the play-by-play,

853
00:58:13,382 --> 00:58:24,645
[SPEAKER_02]: game highlights and boxcarts of every game of the seven games of the 1920 World Series, plus pictures of players at that time and pictures of players in 1953.

854
00:58:25,765 --> 00:58:27,866
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's beautifully done.

855
00:58:27,966 --> 00:58:33,628
[SPEAKER_02]: They're copies of the newspaper stories, I'd say.

856
00:58:33,668 --> 00:58:36,828
[SPEAKER_02]: Kind of Xerox-like, before Xerox was ever thought of.

857
00:58:37,849 --> 00:58:39,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Done on a brown background.

858
00:58:39,789 --> 00:58:42,270
[SPEAKER_00]: What if they were pasted in a scrapbook?

859
00:58:43,294 --> 00:58:46,936
[SPEAKER_02]: It looks very much like it was a scrapbook and photographed.

860
00:58:47,636 --> 00:58:48,937
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's all nicely done.

861
00:58:49,437 --> 00:58:52,399
[SPEAKER_02]: And oh, I just envied that so much.

862
00:58:52,659 --> 00:59:08,567
[SPEAKER_02]: And at the end of our interview, or during the interview, I said, you know, that 20 scrapbook reminds me of my own experience growing up, how I was born the year after, just six months after they won the pennant in the World Series, or seven months maybe.

863
00:59:09,178 --> 00:59:14,359
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'd heard all about that for years and years, and they never would win another pennant until 48.

864
00:59:14,439 --> 00:59:18,580
[SPEAKER_02]: And I just, I was doomed as a boy not to have a pennant there.

865
00:59:19,361 --> 00:59:21,021
[SPEAKER_02]: And we talked a little bit about that.

866
00:59:21,461 --> 00:59:27,463
[SPEAKER_02]: At the end of the thing, I said, that was a great book, I wish I could get a copy of that.

867
00:59:27,503 --> 00:59:36,765
[SPEAKER_02]: Meaning, I hoped that I could talk to these people at the National True Fit Screw Products Corporation in Cleveland, see if I could get another copy.

868
00:59:36,805 --> 00:59:37,185
[SPEAKER_02]: Take it.

869
00:59:39,849 --> 00:59:41,650
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, take it.

870
00:59:41,850 --> 00:59:45,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Rita tried to discourage him, but I had shut her up in a hurry.

871
00:59:45,791 --> 00:59:50,893
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I asked him to write in it, and he wrote in the front there, to Gene from Milt Gaston, with all good wishes.

872
00:59:51,273 --> 00:59:55,094
[SPEAKER_02]: So this is really a coup.

873
00:59:55,134 --> 00:59:57,655
[SPEAKER_02]: But I'm the only person to save her with a copy of this.

874
01:00:00,336 --> 01:00:04,017
[SPEAKER_02]: So I've had a lot of scrapbooks in the last few months about Cleveland Indians.

875
01:00:04,477 --> 01:00:06,758
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Mrs. Gaston had a stroke.

876
01:00:07,859 --> 01:00:08,759
[SPEAKER_00]: She was a little shaky.

877
01:00:10,560 --> 01:00:12,221
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a picture of Mrs. Cranmer.

878
01:00:12,241 --> 01:00:13,501
[SPEAKER_00]: There are a couple of pictures.

879
01:00:13,521 --> 01:00:17,043
[SPEAKER_02]: There's one which says November 23, 1951.

880
01:00:17,143 --> 01:00:18,644
[SPEAKER_02]: It must be love.

881
01:00:18,724 --> 01:00:28,549
[SPEAKER_02]: It's Milt Gasson, a beautiful looking gal, which I think must be Mrs. Gasson, although she looks so much different today because of this stroke we think she may have had.

882
01:00:29,689 --> 01:00:30,910
[SPEAKER_02]: Here's one from 1970.

883
01:00:30,930 --> 01:00:34,972
[SPEAKER_02]: She's looking more like she does now.

884
01:00:35,792 --> 01:00:37,313
[SPEAKER_02]: She's grey and she's drawn.

885
01:00:37,939 --> 01:00:42,180
[SPEAKER_02]: and her emotions are a little bit more strained.

886
01:00:43,941 --> 01:00:50,222
[SPEAKER_02]: But Milt Gaston, he's looking, he shows his age of course.

887
01:00:50,242 --> 01:00:56,804
[SPEAKER_02]: He reminds me a little bit of Pinky Shoffner in his build and in the facial structure.

888
01:00:57,764 --> 01:01:01,145
[SPEAKER_02]: I think he was about this age too that time I saw him.

889
01:01:04,845 --> 01:01:11,468
[SPEAKER_02]: We went into his bedroom after the interview and I took some pictures there.

890
01:01:12,808 --> 01:01:28,354
[SPEAKER_02]: I took some pictures on my 35mm camera of pictures he had framed on the walls of he and his brother, one of Babe Ruth, one of him, I forgot what the other one was.

891
01:01:29,494 --> 01:01:31,215
[SPEAKER_02]: It was Moe Berg, I believe it was one.

892
01:01:31,615 --> 01:01:47,505
[SPEAKER_02]: And he brought out a little scrapbook, which was really more than a scrapbook, it was a photograph album of eight by ten pictures of players he had known and himself, neatly mounted with cellophane wrappers in each page.

893
01:01:47,645 --> 01:01:49,626
[SPEAKER_02]: And I took a few pictures from there too.

894
01:01:49,646 --> 01:01:53,989
[SPEAKER_02]: There'll be another, a better one of himself and his brother Alex.

895
01:01:55,049 --> 01:01:57,190
[SPEAKER_02]: But I didn't take that because they were running out of film.

896
01:01:57,230 --> 01:01:58,211
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd taken another one.

897
01:01:58,251 --> 01:01:59,212
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't know he had this one.

898
01:01:59,232 --> 01:01:59,732
[SPEAKER_02]: I would have taken it.

899
01:01:59,772 --> 01:02:01,293
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a better picture than the one I took.

900
01:02:02,802 --> 01:02:13,227
[SPEAKER_02]: And there was a loose one, kind of a sepia picture tucked into this album, and I took a picture of that and he said, that's my favorite.

901
01:02:13,388 --> 01:02:25,194
[SPEAKER_02]: And I told him then that if this came out, I hope it will, I'd be glad to send him a few copies because he gets all these inquiries from kids, you know, he doesn't have any pictures to send them.

902
01:02:26,662 --> 01:02:31,404
[SPEAKER_02]: happy and willing to respond to kids' letters asking for autographs and pictures.

903
01:02:31,444 --> 01:02:33,205
[SPEAKER_02]: He just doesn't have any pictures.

904
01:02:34,706 --> 01:02:39,148
[SPEAKER_02]: He's the most congenial, friendly, honest person.

905
01:02:39,268 --> 01:02:50,052
[SPEAKER_02]: I now recall a player who was so willing to admit he was wrong in things he did or said, in his performance.

906
01:02:53,013 --> 01:02:57,254
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't want to bring up his lifetime record, which was 97 victories and about 160 defeats.

907
01:02:58,414 --> 01:03:02,215
[SPEAKER_02]: But as he said, he was with a losing team all the time.

908
01:03:02,375 --> 01:03:08,217
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd been with the Yankees or... What was he talking about, the year he had been earned running average in the 70s?

909
01:03:08,237 --> 01:03:09,897
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the George Earnshaw.

910
01:03:11,357 --> 01:03:13,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, his earning average wasn't that good that year.

911
01:03:13,458 --> 01:03:14,358
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't want to bring it up.

912
01:03:14,378 --> 01:03:18,399
[SPEAKER_02]: But his earning averages were much higher in those years.

913
01:03:20,387 --> 01:03:24,570
[SPEAKER_02]: They've come down steadily with this, uh, platoon pitching.

914
01:03:25,971 --> 01:03:29,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh, well, can we sign off now?

915
01:03:31,556 --> 01:03:33,257
[SPEAKER_02]: No, we've got half a tape here to go.

916
01:03:33,277 --> 01:03:34,558
[SPEAKER_02]: We've got 50 minutes to go.

917
01:03:34,578 --> 01:03:36,740
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I just don't know if I can make that.

918
01:03:38,701 --> 01:03:39,862
[SPEAKER_02]: We gotta...

919
01:03:39,963 --> 01:03:41,244
[SPEAKER_02]: I never thought about it.

920
01:03:42,144 --> 01:03:42,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, uh...

921
01:03:46,207 --> 01:03:47,907
[SPEAKER_02]: So this is Thursday night.

922
01:03:47,967 --> 01:03:49,548
[SPEAKER_02]: We've been on our trip about a week.

923
01:03:50,328 --> 01:03:52,108
[SPEAKER_02]: We left Thursday morning a week ago.

924
01:03:52,368 --> 01:03:54,048
[SPEAKER_02]: That makes it about eight days, really.

925
01:03:54,649 --> 01:03:56,209
[SPEAKER_02]: We've been in sunny Florida here.

926
01:03:56,689 --> 01:04:02,630
[SPEAKER_02]: This day on Thursday, January 3rd, 1980, was a glorious day.

927
01:04:03,430 --> 01:04:08,731
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'm sure if we were camping tonight, we'd thought about it, but it was a little late to...

928
01:04:08,771 --> 01:04:11,032
[SPEAKER_00]: It was after five when we left the gas station.

929
01:04:11,052 --> 01:04:13,492
[SPEAKER_02]: There was a campground here in Sarasota.

930
01:04:14,043 --> 01:04:16,945
[SPEAKER_02]: which we thought of going to, but it was late.

931
01:04:17,205 --> 01:04:20,047
[SPEAKER_02]: The way we set up tent last night, you know, it was about 20 minutes.

932
01:04:21,128 --> 01:04:23,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Yesterday afternoon, it was about noon, we had to get to Busch Gardens.

933
01:04:24,231 --> 01:04:25,812
[SPEAKER_02]: I never saw a tent go up so fast.

934
01:04:26,112 --> 01:04:29,074
[SPEAKER_02]: We must have set a world record.

935
01:04:29,114 --> 01:04:31,316
[SPEAKER_02]: We could have done that, I'm sure.

936
01:04:31,356 --> 01:04:33,317
[SPEAKER_02]: We were weary from last night.

937
01:04:33,357 --> 01:04:34,498
[SPEAKER_02]: It was a hard day today.

938
01:04:35,079 --> 01:04:37,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Exhilarating as it was, it was a hard day.

939
01:04:37,020 --> 01:04:40,963
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of driving and finding places to go to.

940
01:04:41,907 --> 01:04:46,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you supposed to get cold tonight?

941
01:04:46,890 --> 01:04:47,971
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

942
01:04:48,671 --> 01:04:51,293
[SPEAKER_02]: It's warmer here than way up north in Tampa.

943
01:04:51,313 --> 01:04:54,995
[SPEAKER_00]: You should have explained about the oranges.

944
01:04:56,196 --> 01:04:59,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you've got a few minutes here if you want to explain.

945
01:04:59,698 --> 01:05:00,919
[SPEAKER_02]: When there's a frost, there's an orange frost.

946
01:05:00,939 --> 01:05:04,621
[SPEAKER_02]: She was not talking about what our neighbor told us last night, Timmy's father.

947
01:05:04,641 --> 01:05:09,064
[SPEAKER_00]: They have smudge pots going and they have air blowing on them.

948
01:05:10,156 --> 01:05:12,262
[SPEAKER_00]: to beat the frost off again.

